WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:02.440 --> 00:00:06.240
That's the sound of Hong Kong residents
booing the Chinese national anthem

00:00:06.240 --> 00:00:12.440
as it plays before a soccer match on a Tuesday night, which is an illegal act in mainland China.

00:00:18.540 --> 00:00:25.280
After more than three months of sustained protests, nobody seems quite sure what will happen next.

00:00:25.280 --> 00:00:28.860
But the protestors we talked to on the march to the soccer stadium

00:00:28.860 --> 00:00:33.060
were clear that the government's formal withdrawal of the controversial extradition bill

00:00:33.060 --> 00:00:36.720
that sparked the protests to begin with, wouldn't be enough.

00:01:25.460 --> 00:01:29.640
The protestors say that their police force has lost public trust,

00:01:29.640 --> 00:01:32.540
and that their government is hopelessly compromised,

00:01:32.540 --> 00:01:35.700
it's leaders controlled by the Chinese Communist Party.

00:01:35.700 --> 00:01:41.660
They want an independent investigation into the police. And they want to elect their own leaders,

00:01:41.660 --> 00:01:47.040
rather than having them selected by interest groups that they view as beholden to mainland China.

00:02:02.300 --> 00:02:07.600
The Hong Kong government is obviously controlled totally by the communist government.

00:02:07.600 --> 00:02:14.420
So that is something that has serviced
within these three months of protests.

00:02:14.420 --> 00:02:20.340
Where we have seen Carrie Lam really
having her hands tied, we have a chief executive

00:02:20.340 --> 00:02:26.580
who is only responding to the requests of the Beijing government.

00:02:26.580 --> 00:02:31.440
Imagery of protesters holding American
flags while marching outside the U.S. consulate

00:02:31.440 --> 00:02:34.240
has made its way into international press coverage.

00:02:34.240 --> 00:02:38.620
And while there weren't any stars and stripes present at Tuesday night's march,

00:02:38.620 --> 00:02:41.580
participants did have a message for Americans.

00:02:53.820 --> 00:02:55.060
Protestors expressed hope

00:02:55.060 --> 00:02:59.820
that the U.S. Congress would pass the Hong Kong Human Rights and Democracy Act,

00:02:59.820 --> 00:03:03.820
which would make the city's bilateral trade and travel agreements with America

00:03:03.820 --> 00:03:07.620
contingent on China maintaining Hong Kong's autonomy,

00:03:07.620 --> 00:03:13.860
and levy sanctions against Chinese individuals accused of violating the rights of Hong Kong citizens.

00:03:13.860 --> 00:03:18.840
It would also instruct U.S. immigration officials not to punish visa applicants

00:03:18.840 --> 00:03:22.000
who've been arrested for participating in the protests.

00:03:22.000 --> 00:03:28.140
Hong Kong's soccer team lost the match, but for many in this crowd, tonight was about more than just a game.

